Here are the limits of each function at $x=-2$, according to the graphs:

$\begin{align}
&\blueD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -2}g(x)=5}
\\\\
&\maroonD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -2}h(x)=1}
\end{align}$

Now we can find $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -2}\dfrac{g(x)}{h(x)}$ using the fact that the limit of a quotient of functions is the quotient of the individual limits (as long as the limit in the denominator isn't equal to $0$):

$\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -2}\dfrac{g(x)}{h(x)}=\dfrac{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -2}g(x)}{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -2}h(x)}$
